# KiCad footprints for the blinky example.
#
#   manta export --format kicad --footprint-map blinky.fpmap blinky.mantaNets
#
# The parts name packages, not libraries: '@~footprint = R-0603' says what the
# part is, and nothing about what any one EDA tool calls it. This file is the
# translation, so the same library also exports to Altium, OrCAD and Allegro
# without editing a part. Every entry must be 'Library:Footprint'; manta
# rejects one that names no library. Names are from KiCad's library, version 9.

R-0603          Resistor_SMD:R_0603_1608Metric
C-0603          Capacitor_SMD:C_0603_1608Metric
C-0805          Capacitor_SMD:C_0805_2012Metric
LED-0603        LED_SMD:LED_0603_1608Metric
LED-KPA-3010    LED_SMD:LED_Kingbright_KPA-3010_3x2x1mm
SOT-23-5        Package_TO_SOT_SMD:SOT-23-5
SOT-23-6        Package_TO_SOT_SMD:SOT-23-6
TSSOP-20        Package_SO:TSSOP-20_4.4x6.5mm_P0.65mm
DIP-2-SMD       Button_Switch_SMD:SW_DIP_SPSTx02_Slide_6.7x6.64mm_W8.61mm_P2.54mm_LowProfile
HDR-1x04        Connector_PinHeader_2.54mm:PinHeader_1x04_P2.54mm_Vertical
HDR-1x05        Connector_PinHeader_2.54mm:PinHeader_1x05_P2.54mm_Vertical
TP-PAD          TestPoint:TestPoint_Pad_D1.5mm

# A footprint's pads have to be the pins the part declares. A real USB-C
# receptacle has pads named A1, B12 and so on, and manta pin numbers are
# integers, so CONN-USB-C declares seven numbered positions and maps to a
# seven-pin header. A board with a genuine receptacle would declare its pad
# numbering in the part, which is a question about the part, not about export.
USB-C-7         Connector_PinHeader_2.54mm:PinHeader_1x07_P2.54mm_Vertical
